l erosion  he says climate change has contributed  significantly to the rising waters along  ghana's shoreline  destructive waves have become more  frequent  he fears that they are also a threat for  communities behind the coastline  the space between the lagoon and the sea  is being narrowed the sea is about to  enter the last tidal wave that okay  the sea went into the lagoon the waves  went through town the remaining town and  went into the lagoon a sign that in few  years to come the sea may take over  those places so there is a great danger  ahead  defenses like these have been built to  protect some communities  but the government says there is not  enough money to build sea walls to  protect everyone  rafal abanavo is the local  representative for this district he's  inspecting the damage done to fuvemei  the one who tells him he feels let down  by the government  agbanovo admits more needs to be done to  protect the community  and the environment  it's not a matter of even protecting the  people per se but look at the natural  environment  the vegetation water bodies have been  destroyed by the sea because we have a  lagoon back of this community you have  mangrove back of this community coconut  trees you lost as many as seven thousand  there is only the shell left standing of  knowledge there were no childhood home  he and thousands like him will have to  find new ones  with no certainty that they want to lose  them again to the waves consuming  ghana's coastline  for more on this i'm now joined by  professor christie pienenado he's the  director institute for environment and  sanitation studies university of ghana  hello and welcome to the program  professor you've researched the  development of ghana's coasts from  historic records since the late 19th  century what developments did you  discover over the years  thank you very much and hello to your  cherished listeners and viewers  what we identified is that erosion is  not new it's something that has been  there for a long time  but then the challenge is that  development has grown so close  to the shoreline and therefore  interacting with the natural dynamics of  the system  and because of the the interaction  between human development and the  natural dynamics of the shoreline that  is what is creating the problems and  hence the enhanced impact of erosion  undeveloped communities and then  people around  how how bad has it gotten  it's really  it has become extremely bad in the sense  that  we have lots of people now being  affected we have  several areas of the coastal communities  also being affected especially the  vulnerable areas  and  it's really affecting the livelihoods of  people who live within the coastal  communities  and  by extension also affecting  the the economy because once the  livelihood of people are affected then  the economy is also affected so  there are all sorts of factors at play  here but what is the main driving force  behind coastal erosion  several factors as you rightly said can  be attributed to coastal erosion they  are natural factors and then also human  induced  factors the natural factors there isn't  much you can do about it  but the human induced factors are what  we can  do something about it  what we observed is that in recent times  the intensity and severity has increased  as a result of storm such  being driven by climate change  and then we also have  increasing um  activities with regards to uh intensive  wave but wave wave wave activities and  then we also identified the increasing  sea level  rise which has also resulted in some of  these  activities so these are some of the  natural factors driven by climate change  where somebody can also say that climate  change is human induced  well  it's true but these are some of the  natural factors that are driving erosion  along our coast in terms of human  activities we can talk about sand  winning  and although there is a ban on some  winning but it is not being enforced so  people indiscriminately  uh wind sand on the beaches we also have  destruction of coastal vegetation and  these coastal vegetation are very vital  in naturally fighting erosion so these  are some of the factors that i can  easily talk about and we can also talk  about sedimentation once you  have influenced the sediment dynamics  the sediments the the the sand that are  along the shoreline once you influence  the segment dynamics then you are  bound to have some of these activities  occurring so clearly something should be  done about it we've had criticism of the  government's coastal management plan can  i help at all or what's the way forward  yes  definitely something needs to be done  about it but the way we go about it is  critical  presently the government is using hard  engineering approach  where they try to  manage erosion by what i call fighting  erosion  so they put you know  harsh structures like  sea walls and then groins  and the whole idea is to intercept  sediment and help to build beaches in  areas where they are eroded but then as  you trap sediment to build beaches you  deprive the downdraft side of sediment  so it's like robbing peter to pay paul  in trying to solve a problem here you  create a bigger problem down downstream  so the method that the government has  been using over the years are not  sustainable so we need to rethink as a  country how we can effectively manage  erosion but not fight erosion  okay professor chrysia pneunado from the  university of ghana thank you very much  for the insights  it's a pleasure thank you  you
 

